            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to prove wrong, then the Latin for a guess."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: concēdō, concēdere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "concēdō, concēdere — \"to grant, to concede; to give way\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hoc tibi concēdō, sed nōn omnia. I grant you this, but not everything."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "concēdō, concēdere, concessī, concessum. Dative of the person, accusative of the point: hoc tibi concēdō. English concede and concession."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "concēdō is built from con- and cēdō \"to go, to yield\" (Lewis & Short): to step aside for someone. English concede comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
